ages/states/numayrids.jpg","cr":{"a":"Attar-Aram syria","l":"CC BY-SA 4.0","s":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Numayrid_dynasty.png"}},"natalia-republic":{"img":"/images/states/natalia-republic.jpg","cr":{"a":"Himasaram","l":"Public domain","s":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Natalia_Republic.svg"}},"nagaoka-domain":{"img":"/images/states/nagaoka-domain.jpg","cr":{"a":"Barfodt","l":"CC0","s":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_the_Makino_Lords_of_Nagaoka_in_Echigo_Province_(B).svg"}},"navajo-nation":{"img":"/images/states/navajo-nation.jpg","cr":{"a":"Jay R. Degroat","l":"Public domain","s":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Official_design_of_the_Navajo_Nation_flag_as_it_was_adopted_on_May_21,_1968.svg"}},"northern-rhodesia":{"img":"/images/states/northern-rhodesia.jpg","cr":{"a":"Greentubing (en:WP talk)","l":"Public domain","s":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Northern_Rhodesia_(1939%E2%80%931964).svg"}},"northern-ireland":{"img":"/images/states/northern-ireland.jpg","cr":{"a":"Blank map of Europe (with disputed regions).svg: maix derivative work Alphathon /\u0027æɫfə.θɒn/","l":"CC BY-SA 3.0","s":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Northern_Ireland_in_the_UK_and_Europe.svg"}},"nanzan":{"img":"/images/states/nanzan.jpg","cr":{"a":"user:kallgan","l":"CC BY-SA 3.0","s":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ryukyu_Kingdoms_of_Sanzan_era.jpg"}},"negeri-sembilan":{"img":"/images/states/negeri-sembilan.jpg","cr":{"a":"TUBS","l":"CC BY-SA 3.0","s":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Negeri_Sembilan_in_Malaysia.svg"}},"nagnatae":{"t":"People of north-west Ireland recorded by Ptolemy, who also names a notable town of theirs called Nagnata.","o":"Ptolemy\u0027s Geography of about 150 places the Nagnatae on the north-west coast of Ireland, roughly in Sligo or north Mayo, and lists a settlement called Nagnata, which he describes as a notable town. His information came from earlier sailors\u0027 reports on the Atlantic coast.","r":"Nothing is recorded of their rulers, economy or customs. The identification of Nagnata is disputed; sites around Sligo Bay have been suggested, and some versions of the text spell the people Magnatae, which has led to other proposed meanings. The region was rich in older monuments such as the Carrowmore tombs.","e":"No later source mentions the Nagnatae. Their area later lay within the Kingdom of Connacht, ruled by branches of the Connachta dynasties.","re":"Celtic polytheism","la":"Goidelic Celtic","c":"low","img":"/images/states/nagnatae.jpg","cr":{"a":"Nicolaus Germanus","l":"Public domain","s":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ptolemy_Cosmographia_1467_-_Ireland.jpg"}},"nagar-state":{"img":"/images/states/nagar-state.jpg","cr":{"a":"Ali Nagari","l":"CC BY-SA 4.0","s":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Nagar_State_Flag.png"}},"ngo-dynasty":{"img":"/images/states/ngo-dynasty.jpg","cr":{"a":"TRMC","l":"CC BY-SA 4.0","s":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Map_of_T%C4%A9nh_H%E1%BA%A3i_qu%C3%A2n_and_early_years_of_Ng%C3%B4_dynasty.png"}},"nazca":{"img":"/images/states/nazca.jpg","cr":{"a":"Murray Foubister","l":"CC BY-SA 2.0","s":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Plaza_de_Armas,_Nazca_(8443289015).jpg"}},"najahid-dynasty":{"img":"/images/states/najahid-dynasty.jpg","cr":{"a":"Unknown authorUnknown author","l":"Public domain","s":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Najahid_coin.jpg"}},"nanyue":{"img":"/images/states/nanyue.jpg","cr":{"a":"People\u0027s_Republic_of_China_(orthographic_projection).svg: Ssolbergj (talk) derivative work: 长夜无风 (talk)、玖巧仔 (talk)","l":"CC BY-SA 3.0","s":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Nanyue.svg"}},"nawabate-of-bengal":{"img":"/images/states/nawabate-of-bengal.jpg","cr":{"a":"","l":"Public domain","s":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Murshid_Quli_Jafar_Khan.jpg"}},"nembe-kingdom":{"img":"/images/states/nembe-kingdom.jpg","cr":{"a":"CommonRollebon (talk) and commons","l":"CC BY-SA 3.0","s":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:IjawHistory.jpeg"}},"nuceria-alfaterna":{"img":"/images/states/nuceria-alfaterna.jpg","cr":{"a":"Poul Krogsgård","l":"Public domain","s":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Nocera1.jpg"}},"northern-han":{"img":"/images/states/northern-han.jpg","cr":{"a":"Wangyefurichard","l":"CC BY-SA 3.0","s":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Northern_Han_Kingdom,_951.svg"}},"nesiotic-league":{"t":"League of the Cycladic islands centred on Delos, created by Antigonus I and used by Hellenistic kings to control the Aegean.","o":"Around 314 BCE Antigonus I Monophthalmus, seeking support in the Aegean against rival successors of Alexander, organised the islands of the Cyclades into a league with its headquarters at the sanctuary of Apollo on Delos. Members celebrated festivals named after Antigonus and his son Demetrius.","r":"Delegates of the islands met in a federal council (synedrion) on Delos, voted honours for royal patrons and handled common affairs, but real power lay with the protecting king. After the collapse of Demetrius, the league passed under Ptolemaic control in the 280s BCE; officials called nesiarchs and Philocles, king of Sidon, acted for Ptolemy II, and the islanders founded the Ptolemaia festival.","e":"Ptolemaic power in the Aegean waned in the mid-3rd century BCE and the league faded. It was revived around 200 BCE under Rhodian leadership, but ended in 167 BCE when Rome gave Delos to Athens and declared it a free port, ending Rhodian dominance.","ru":["Antigonus I Monophthalmus","Ptolemy II Philadelphus"],"re":"Ancient Greek religion (cult of Apollo)","la":"Ancient Greek","c":"medium","img":"/images/states/nesiotic-league.jpg","cr":{"a":"Cplakidas","l":"CC BY 3.0","s":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Cyclades.svg"}},"northern-qi":{"img":"/images/states/northern-qi.jpg","cr":{"a":"naturalearthdata.com, offered to the Public Domain per Terms of Use","l":"CC0","s":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Map_of_the_Northern_Qi.png"}},"new-republic-brazil":{"img":"/images/states/new-republic-brazil.jpg","cr":{"a":"Senado Federal","l":"CC BY 2.0","s":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Diretas_J%C3%A1_(4987732126).jpg"}},"nervii":{"t":"The fiercest of the Belgic peoples, who banned wine and luxuries and almost defeated Caesar at the Battle of the Sabis in 57 BCE.","o":"The Nervii lived in the Hainaut region of modern Belgium and northern France, between the Scheldt and the Sambre. Caesar reports that they forbade merchants and wine, believing luxuries weakened courage, and that they were proud of their Germanic descent.","r":"They had no cavalry to speak of and fought on foot, using thick hedges of bent saplings to block their land against raiders. Their war leader in 57 BCE was Boduognatus.","e":"At the Sabis (probably the Sambre) in 57 BCE they surprised Caesar\u0027s camp and were crushed only after a desperate battle. In 54 BCE they besieged Quintus Cicero\u0027s winter camp. Under Rome their chief town was Bagacum (Bavay), later Cambrai.","ru":["Boduognatus"],"re":"Celtic polytheism","la":"Gaulish","img":"/images/states/nervii.jpg","cr":{"a":"","l":"CC BY-SA 3.0","s":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Gaule_Belgique.png"}},"nippur":{"img":"/images/states/nippur.jpg","cr":{"a":"Jasmine N. Walthall, U.S. Army","l":"Public domain","s":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ruins_from_a_temple_in_Naffur.jpg"}},"numidia":{"img":"/images/states/numidia.jpg","cr":{"a":"Masinissa (0238?-0148 av. J.-C. ; roi de Numidie). Autorité émettrice de monnaie présumée Micipsa. Autorité émettrice...","l":"Public domain","s":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Monnaie_-_Bronze,_Incertain,_Numidie,_Massinissa_-_btv1b84804368_(2_of_2).jpg"}},"north-caucasian-soviet-republic":{"img":"/images/states/north-caucasian-soviet-republic.jpg","cr":{"a":"User:R-41","l":"Public domain","s":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Socialist_red_flag.svg"}},"